# Contributing to @DISPLAY@

Use this guide for the first day and ordinary changes. Keep changes focused,
English-only and tested. You own this application; there is no required connection
to the original starter. [MAINTENANCE.md](MAINTENANCE.md) covers dependencies,
toolchains, security tooling, releases and recovery; [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md)
covers private vulnerability reports. Preserve the [MIT notice](LICENSE).

## 1. Own your repository

Start in the freshly generated application's root. Confirm you are in the right
folder and it contains no inherited `.git` directory before initializing. The
output is owned source, not a Git fork. Review `.gitignore` and inspect staged files:

```sh
git init
git add .
git diff --cached --stat
git commit -m "Initial application"
```

If Git asks for author identity, configure your own name/email using Git's normal
settings, preferably local to this repository when appropriate; do not copy someone
else's identity. No remote is added automatically. Choose your own host/repository
and follow that host's connection instructions. No upstream starter remote is
needed. GitHub is optional for local work; the supplied GitHub Actions workflows
need equivalent configuration if moving to another CI provider.

Local `.env`, databases/sidecars, `data`, `target`, `node_modules`, `dist`, browser
reports, backups and security reports are ignored. `.env.example` is intentionally
trackable and must contain examples only. Ignore rules are not a secret scanner:
review `git status --short` and staged files before every commit. Never force-add
secrets, private keys, production data or generated outputs.

## 2. Run and understand the application

Follow [README quick starts](README.md) for tools and first startup. Examples use a
POSIX shell; on Windows use WSL or adapt environment-variable syntax in your shell.
Run native Cargo commands from `backend`, npm commands from `frontend`, and Python
project scripts/Compose from the application root unless shown otherwise.

| Mode | Browser/assets | API and configuration | Database |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Native Rust | Last `frontend/dist` build at port 8080 | `backend/src/main.rs` -> `lib.rs` -> routes; `PORT` defaults to 8080 | `backend/data/@NAME@.db` if launched from backend |
| Vite development | React/TypeScript hot reload on 5173; no production bundle required in the browser | `frontend/vite.config.ts` proxies `/api` and `/health` to 127.0.0.1:8080, configurable with `API_PROXY_TARGET` | Same Rust backend/database, not a Vite database |
| Docker/production | Rust serves compiled assets from `/app/public` | One process; container 8080, loopback host publication controlled by `HOST_PORT` | Named volume mounted at `/data/@NAME@.db` |

Vite and native Rust listen on all interfaces by default; use a trusted development
environment. Docker's default published address is loopback. No mode supplies
authentication. The Rust server still needs the initial `dist` build even when
browsing Vite. `npm run dev` updates the development browser; run `npm run build`
again to update the assets served by native Rust or a new production image.

For another native backend port, in `backend` use `PORT=8081 cargo run --locked`;
in the frontend terminal use `API_PROXY_TARGET=http://127.0.0.1:8081 npm run dev`.
This is different from Docker `HOST_PORT=8081`. Restart Rust after source/config
changes. `.env.example` is not a Rust dotenv loader; Compose may separately use
`.env` for interpolation. Defaults/validation live in
[config.rs](backend/src/config.rs); do not diagnose by dumping all environment values.

The application shares a SQLx pool through `AppState` in [lib.rs](backend/src/lib.rs).
[db.rs](backend/src/db.rs) prepares SQLite, applies embedded migrations before
serving, and enables WAL/FKs/FULL synchronization with bounded timeouts. Missing
native parent directories are created when permissions allow; an unwritable parent
is an error, not a reason to reset the database. SQLITE_PATH must be an ordinary
file path, not `:memory:` or a `file:` URI (the Python inspection URI below is a
separate client interface).

## 3. Make a small backend change

[Route registration](backend/src/routes/mod.rs) maps `GET /api/hello` to
[hello.rs](backend/src/routes/hello.rs). That handler extracts `State<AppState>`,
queries `state.pool` with a bound parameter, and serializes the nearby `Greeting`
response type. Keep small request/response types beside their handler; introduce
modules when needed, not a mandatory service/repository layer. Errors use
[error.rs](backend/src/error.rs), with safe public responses and sanitized logs.

**Illustrative opt-in edit, not present in the baseline:** to change only greeting
presentation, replace the success arm `Ok(Ok(message)) => Ok(Json(Greeting { message })),`
inside `hello` with:

```rust
Ok(Ok(message)) => Ok(Json(Greeting {
    message: format!("Welcome: {message}"),
})),
```

The route, state access and database schema stay the same. Do not edit an applied
seed migration to achieve this presentation change. Update the HTTP expectations
in [routes.rs](backend/tests/routes.rs): both the initial greeting and the later
`A changed greeting` response now have the `Welcome: ` prefix. Keep raw database
expectations unchanged. Add a regression for the behavior you choose and retain
error/404/write-rejection tests. For broader verification, update relevant native,
browser and Docker HTTP expectations too; search with `git grep -n 'Hello from'`
and inspect assertions rather than blindly replacing database fixture text. Also
review non-default changed-message HTTP assertions; do not rewrite raw SQLite checks.

From `backend`, validate the focused API tests, then all Rust checks:

```sh
cargo fmt --all -- --check
cargo test --locked --test routes
cargo check --locked
cargo clippy --locked --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings
cargo test --locked
cargo build --locked
```

Restart `cargo run --locked` to inspect your endpoint with
`curl --fail http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/hello`. This documentation does not change the
shipped API; choosing the example is your own application change.

## 4. Make a small frontend change

[index.html](frontend/index.html) is Vite's HTML entry; it loads
[main.tsx](frontend/src/main.tsx), which mounts React's `App` and imports
[styles.css](frontend/src/styles.css). The current component is
[App.tsx](frontend/src/App.tsx); no `components` or `pages` directory exists yet.
[api.ts](frontend/src/api.ts) fetches `/api/hello` and `/health/ready`, validates
response shapes, and returns typed data. Do not duplicate fetch logic just to edit
visible text or add a state-management library for this example.

**Illustrative opt-in edit:** in App.tsx, change the heading `Your stack, connected`
to `Your application, connected`. In [App.test.tsx](frontend/src/App.test.tsx), add
this assertion to the existing test after rendering App:

```tsx
expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Your application, connected' })).toBeInTheDocument()
```

Use the running Vite terminal to see the edit. Keep tests colocated with components;
Vitest currently discovers `src/**/*.test.tsx`. From `frontend`:

```sh
npm run typecheck
npm run lint
npm test
npm run build
```

These validate types, ESLint, mocked-browser unit behavior and production assets.
They do not substitute for real backend/browser smoke tests. API changes should be
reflected in api.ts types/validation and relevant tests, not hidden with `any`.

## 5. Add a migration safely

The existing history is [0001_greeting.sql](backend/migrations/0001_greeting.sql)
in [backend/migrations](backend/migrations). Choose a new unused numeric version
larger than all existing ones and a descriptive underscore-separated filename.
SQLx orders by numeric version, not file modification time. The following is an
**illustrative new file**, not an existing migration or a required feature:

```sql
-- Example only: 0002_example.sql, if version 2 is unused.
CREATE TABLE example_notes (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
    body TEXT NOT NULL
);
```

Do not change existing SQL/checksums or delete `_sqlx_migrations` rows. Migrations
are embedded at compilation; [build.rs](backend/build.rs) watches the directory.
Rebuild and restart the backend: pending migrations run before HTTP traffic, and
SQLx records versions, success and checksums in `_sqlx_migrations`. A copied SQL
file alone does not update an already built container. Startup does not provide
automatic migration reversal.

Before schema changes, use the [maintenance/recovery guide](MAINTENANCE.md#releases-updates-and-recovery)
to protect and prove recovery of existing data. Test both a fresh disposable database
and a retained disposable copy with known data. Record versions/checksums and data
before the change; after rebuilding/restarting, verify the old checksum/data remain
and only the intended new migration appears. Do not infer data preservation from a
healthy endpoint alone. Update tests that intentionally assert the baseline migration
count, while keeping integrity and existing-history preservation assertions.

For the default native path, from a second terminal in `backend`, after starting
the app, inspect a database without creating a missing one:

```sh
python3 - <<'PY'
from pathlib import Path
import sqlite3
path = Path('data/@NAME@.db').resolve()
with sqlite3.connect(path.as_uri() + '?mode=ro', uri=True) as connection:
    print(connection.execute('PRAGMA integrity_check').fetchone()[0])
    print(connection.execute('SELECT version,success,hex(checksum) FROM _sqlx_migrations ORDER BY version').fetchall())
PY
```

Use the actual configured path if you changed it, and inspect data locally rather
than publishing database contents. The SQL `mode=ro` avoids accidental empty-db
creation; the native data directory must still support SQLite WAL sidecars. For
container snapshots/restores use the documented helpers, not live-file copying.

From `backend`, `python3 ../scripts/migration-build-smoke.py` checks incremental
embedding using its own disposable source/database and a new probe migration. It
requires dependencies/toolchains already installed. Also run database/API tests and
recovery checks appropriate to your schema change; that build regression is not a
complete application-specific upgrade test.

## 6. Verification and review

From the application root, before builds:

```sh
python3 scripts/check-toolchain-pins.py
python3 scripts/test-toolchain-pins.py -v
```

Use the Rust/frontend checks above and locked installs from README. Run process
checks from `backend` after both assets and the debug binary are built:

```sh
python3 ../scripts/test-smoke-paths.py -v
python3 ../scripts/backend-smoke.py
```

For real browser checks, from `frontend` after the backend/asset build:

```sh
npx playwright install --with-deps chromium
python3 ../scripts/browser-smoke.py
```

`CHROMIUM_PATH` may select an installed compatible browser. Process/signal checks
target Unix; ensure builds and runners use the same Cargo configuration/cwd when
using `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`. A missing selected binary fails without stale fallback.

For packaging/storage changes, from the project root:

```sh
docker build -t @NAME@:local .
docker compose config --quiet
python3 scripts/docker-smoke.py --image @NAME@:local -v
python3 scripts/sqlite-backup-smoke.py --image @NAME@:local -v
```

Smoke resources are disposable; never substitute a deployment volume. See
[SECURITY-SCANNING.md](SECURITY-SCANNING.md) for audits/installer/network/reporting
limits and [MAINTENANCE.md](MAINTENANCE.md) for risk-based update verification.
Record the actual commands, results and skipped checks in your PR. Do not treat
local results as proof of remote CI or production deployment. Before committing,
inspect diffs and deliberate lockfile changes; never weaken checks to hide failures.

## 7. Troubleshooting

Diagnostics below assume the native cwd or unchanged developer Compose file shown
in README. For released deployments preserve the exact project/files/image selection
from the maintenance runbook. Inspect logs/config locally; do not publish secrets,
private environment values or database exports. Stop if storage identity or schema
compatibility is uncertain.

| Symptom | Likely cause and safe inspection | Next step |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Wrong Rust compiler | From backend: `rustup show active-toolchain`; check `rust-toolchain.toml` and `rustc --version` | Install/select that toolchain through rustup; [maintenance](MAINTENANCE.md#rust-toolchain-and-libraries), not a blind pin edit |
| Cargo locked build/resolution failure | Inspect Cargo.toml/Cargo.lock diff and first compiler/resolution error; `cargo check --locked` | Coordinate intended changes through maintenance; do not delete Cargo.lock or bypass TLS |
| rustfmt failure | `cargo fmt --all -- --check` shows the exact diff; personalized long names such as maintenance-notes have a known pre-alpha config.rs wrapping defect | Review the diff and handle the known source-generation defect in a focused correction; never disable the gate or call it a pin-check regression |
| Rust compile error | Read the first error's file/type/span, then run focused tests | Fix the actual contract/type error; keep error sanitization and [backend tests](backend/tests) |
| Wrong Node/npm | In frontend: `node --version`, `npm --version`; compare .node-version/packageManager and pin checker | Use matching tools; see [version map](MAINTENANCE.md#version-map); bundled npm is not selected automatically by packageManager |
| npm lock mismatch | Review package.json/package-lock.json; `npm ci --no-audit --no-fund` reports inconsistency | Resolve intentional changes with selected npm, review the lock; do not delete integrity fields or force audit fixes |
| Frontend build/type errors | Run `npm run typecheck`, then `npm run build`; inspect the first source error | Fix types/imports/assets; do not hide errors with `any` or skip the build |
| Browser API unavailable | Confirm Rust is running; `curl --fail http://127.0.0.1:8080/health/ready`; inspect api.ts and Vite's proxy target | Match PORT/API_PROXY_TARGET, inspect safe backend logs; a working Vite page does not prove the API is running |
| Native DB path/missing parent | Check cwd and the ordinary-file SQLITE_PATH in config.rs; parent is created only when writable | Use the correct owned local directory; never create/reset a guessed database to hide missing data |
| DB permission/read-only error | Check parent/file ownership; container UID/GID is 10001; root read-only is intentional but /data must be writable | Provision the correct local volume/permissions per [maintenance](MAINTENANCE.md#docker-and-runtime-package-maintenance); no chmod 777 or live sidecar edits |
| Migration error/checksum mismatch | Read safe startup diagnostics and compare immutable SQL with applied version/checksum metadata | Stop and preserve data; restore the correct historical file from your own Git history or plan reviewed recovery; never delete history rows |
| Docker daemon unavailable | `docker info --format '{{.ServerVersion}}'` | Start/fix the intended daemon/context; do not change application data |
| Docker build fails | Inspect the first failed build stage, pins, registry/CA access and free disk | Fix that stage; see [image maintenance](MAINTENANCE.md#docker-and-runtime-package-maintenance); retain known-good image/data |
| Container startup/health fails | `docker compose ps`; `docker compose logs --tail 50 app` | Inspect migration/config/storage errors; readiness queries SQLite; [release/recovery](MAINTENANCE.md#releases-updates-and-recovery) before rollback |
| Port occupied | Stop your own conflicting dev process, or use PORT with matching Vite target / Docker HOST_PORT as appropriate | Recheck the correct URL; do not stop unrelated services |
| Missing persistent data/unexpected volume | `docker compose config --quiet` checks syntax only; privately inspect the actual /data mount as in maintenance | Stop writes and reconcile project/volume/path; never use volume removal or reseeding as a shortcut |
| Pin check fails | `python3 scripts/check-toolchain-pins.py` names the inconsistent declarations | Review the intended relationship and [offline checker limits](MAINTENANCE.md#offline-consistency-check); coordinate legitimate updates |
| Scanner install/DB/network fails | Read status/exit code and [scan setup](SECURITY-SCANNING.md); check pinned version, CA trust, registry access and writable cache | Fix infrastructure with TLS verification intact; an incomplete scan is not clean |
| Vulnerabilities reported | Review advisory/version/feature/vendor/reachability evidence using [triage guidance](SECURITY-SCANNING.md) | Small verified remediation or a separately justified residual-risk decision; no blanket ignores/threshold changes |
